# CITATION.cff for https://github.com/ahillsley/liv_zones
#
# Place this file at the ROOT of the repository, named exactly: CITATION.cff
#
# BEFORE THE FIRST RELEASE, EDIT:
#   - Fill in all ORCID lines (replace 0000-0000-0000-0000 with real ORCIDs,
#     or delete the orcid line entirely if an author has none)
#   - Fill in full names for adjavon(lines marked TODO below)
#   - license: must match the SPDX identifier of the LICENSE file in the repo
#     (if there is no LICENSE file yet, add one — Zenodo and reusers both need it)
#   - date-released: update to the date you publish the GitHub release
#   - version: update to match the git tag you release (e.g. v0.1.0)
#
# Do NOT add a top-level `doi:` field before the first release — Zenodo mints it
# at ingest. After the first release you may add the CONCEPT DOI ("Cite all
# versions"), never a version DOI.
#
# VALIDATE BEFORE COMMITTING:
#   pip install cffconvert
#   cffconvert --validate

cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this code, please cite both this software and the paper listed under preferred-citation."
type: software
title: "liv_zones: Spatial organelle segmentation and mapping pipeline for fluorescence microscopy"
abstract: >-
  liv_zones is a Python pipeline for spatial organellomics analysis of
  fluorescence microscopy images. It segments cells and organelles
  (mitochondria, lipid droplets, peroxisomes) from multi-channel confocal
  z-stacks using Cellpose, extracts per-organelle and per-cell morphological
  features, and maps them spatially along anatomical tissue axes
  (portal-to-central vein in liver; endocrine/exocrine regions in pancreas).
  The pipeline covers image preprocessing (cropping, z-stack max projection,
  image correction filters), cell filtering, feature extraction, and
  generation of spatial trend plots. It supports multi-tissue analysis and
  is extensible to new tissue types via community forks.
version: "0.1.0"
date-released: "2026-04-09"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/ahillsley/liv_zones"
url: "https://ahillsley.github.io/liv_zones/"
keywords:
  - spatial organellomics
  - organelle segmentation
  - fluorescence microscopy
  - liver zonation
  - pancreas
  - mitochondria
  - lipid droplets
  - peroxisomes
  - Cellpose
  - cell state
  - metabolic adaptation
  - Python

